urodevelopmental disorders in a highly consanguineous population\n\n[Tamam Khalaf](/?term=Khalaf+T&cauthor_id=38438125)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Genetic Counseling Division, Igenomix, Dubai, UAE.\") , [Mode Al Ojaimi](/?term=Al+Ojaimi+M&cauthor_id=38438125)  [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")   [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\") , [Dina Amin Saleh](/?term=Saleh+DA&cauthor_id=38438125)  [4](#full-view-affiliation-4 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, American Center for Psychiatry and Neurology, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\")   [5](#full-view-affiliation-5 \"Department of Pediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, Ain Shams University, Cairo, Egypt.\") , [Alena Sulaiman](/?term=Sulaiman+A&cauthor_id=38438125)  [6](#full-view-affiliation-6 \"Pediatric Division, KidsHeart Medical Center, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\") , [Aman P Sohal](/?term=Sohal+AP&cauthor_id=38438125)  [7](#full-view-affiliation-7 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\")   [8](#full-view-affiliation-8 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Al Qassimi Women and Children's Hospital, Sharjah, UAE.\") , [Arif Khan](/?term=Khan+A&cauthor_id=38438125)  [7](#full-view-affiliation-7 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\")   [9](#full-view-affiliation-9 \"Pediatric Division, Kings College Hospital London, Dubai, UAE.\")   [10](#full-view-affiliation-10 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Kids Neuro Clinic and Rehab Center, Dubai, UAE.\") , [Ayman W El-Hattab](/?term=El-Hattab+AW&cauthor_id=38438125)  [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")   [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Genetic Counseling Division, Igenomix, Dubai, UAE.\n-   2 Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   3 Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   4 Pediatric Neurology Division, American Center for Psychiatry and Neurology, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\n-   5 Department of Pediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, Ain Shams University, Cairo, Egypt.\n-   6 Pediatric Division, KidsHeart Medical Center, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\n-   7 Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\n-   8 Pediatric Neurology Division, Al Qassimi Women and Children's Hospital, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   9 Pediatric Division, Kings College Hospital London, Dubai, UAE.\n-   10 Pediatric Neurology Division, Kids Neuro Clinic and Rehab Center, Dubai, UAE.\n\n-   PMID: **38438125**\n-   DOI: [10.1111/cge.14508](https://doi.org/10.1111/cge.14508)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# The utility of exome sequencing in diagnosing pediatric neurodevelopmental disorders in a highly consanguineous population\n\nTamam Khalaf et al. Clin Genet. 2024 Jul.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nClin Genet\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Clin+Genet%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Clin+Genet%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2024 Jul;106(1):82-89.\n\ndoi: 10.1111/cge.14508. Epub 2024 Mar 4.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Tamam Khalaf](/?term=Khalaf+T&cauthor_id=38438125)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Genetic Counseling Division, Igenomix, Dubai, UAE.\") , [Mode Al Ojaimi](/?term=Al+Ojaimi+M&cauthor_id=38438125)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\") , [Dina Amin Saleh](/?term=Saleh+DA&cauthor_id=38438125)  [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, American Center for Psychiatry and Neurology, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\")   [5](#short-view-affiliation-5 \"Department of Pediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, Ain Shams University, Cairo, Egypt.\") , [Alena Sulaiman](/?term=Sulaiman+A&cauthor_id=38438125)  [6](#short-view-affiliation-6 \"Pediatric Division, KidsHeart Medical Center, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\") , [Aman P Sohal](/?term=Sohal+AP&cauthor_id=38438125)  [7](#short-view-affiliation-7 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\")   [8](#short-view-affiliation-8 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Al Qassimi Women and Children's Hospital, Sharjah, UAE.\") , [Arif Khan](/?term=Khan+A&cauthor_id=38438125)  [7](#short-view-affiliation-7 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\")   [9](#short-view-affiliation-9 \"Pediatric Division, Kings College Hospital London, Dubai, UAE.\")   [10](#short-view-affiliation-10 \"Pediatric Neurology Division, Kids Neuro Clinic and Rehab Center, Dubai, UAE.\") , [Ayman W El-Hattab](/?term=El-Hattab+AW&cauthor_id=38438125)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Genetic Counseling Division, Igenomix, Dubai, UAE.\n-   2 Department of Clinical Sciences, College of Medicine, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   3 Department of Pediatrics, University Hospital Sharjah, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   4 Pediatric Neurology Division, American Center for Psychiatry and Neurology, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\n-   5 Department of Pediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, Ain Shams University, Cairo, Egypt.\n-   6 Pediatric Division, KidsHeart Medical Center, Abu Dhabi, UAE.\n-   7 Pediatric Neurology Division, Neuropedia Children's Neuroscience Center, Dubai, UAE.\n-   8 Pediatric Neurology Division, Al Qassimi Women and Children's Hospital, Sharjah, UAE.\n-   9 Pediatric Division, Kings College Hospital London, Dubai, UAE.\n-   10 Pediatric Neurology Division, Kids Neuro Clinic and Rehab Center, Dubai, UAE.\n\n-   PMID: **38438125**\n-   DOI: [10.1111/cge.14508](https://doi.org/10.1111/cge.14508)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nExome sequencing (ES) has been utilized in diagnosing children with neurodevelopmental manifestations, this study aimed to investigate the utility of ES in children within a highly consanguineous population that presented with neurodevelopmental complaints. A retrospective chart review was performed for 405 children with neurodevelopmental complaints who have had ES and were evaluated in multiple centers in the United Arab Emirates over a four-year period. Within the cohort of 405 children, consanguinity was reported in 35% (144/405). The primary clinical presentations were developmental delay/cognitive impairment, distinctive facial features, hypotonia, seizures, and weakness. The diagnostic yield was 57% (231/405). Novel variants were identified in 54% (125/231) of positive cases. Within the positive cases, specific treatment was available in 6% (13/231) and copy number variants (CNV) were reported in 3% (8/231) of cases. In eight children, variants in genes that have not yet been linked to human disease that could potentially be the cause of the observed phenotype \"candidate genes\" were identified. ES was utilized effectively within this cohort with a high diagnostic yield and through the identification of novel gene variants, CNVs, candidate genes and secondary findings as well as the alteration of the treatment plan in cases where treatment was available.\n\n**Keywords:** candidate genes; consanguinity; exome sequencing; neurodevelopmental; novel variants; pediatric.\n\n© 2024 John Wiley & Sons A/S. Publi